Genome-Wide Association Studies (GWAS) for Evolutionary Biology (GWAS01) ? Applications for Ecology

9?13 November 2026 | 25 hours | ?400 | Live Online

Course information and registration<https://prstats.org/course/genome-wide-association-studies-gwas-for-evolutionary-biology-gwas01/>

Delivered by Dr Nikolay Oskolkov, a bioinformatician and computational biologist specialising in genomics, population genetics, bioinformatics, statistical modelling and computational biology.

Learn how to conduct Genome-Wide Association Studies (GWAS) to identify genetic variants associated with complex biological traits, using practical computational workflows for genomic data processing, quality control, association testing, population structure analysis and interpretation of genomic results.

For ecologists, GWAS provides powerful approaches for investigating the genetic basis of ecologically important traits, including environmental tolerance, phenotypic variation, disease resistance, life-history characteristics and adaptation to different habitats and environmental conditions.

These methods are particularly valuable for researchers working in molecular ecology, ecological genomics, conservation genetics and evolutionary ecology, where understanding relationships between genomic variation and phenotypic traits can provide insights into adaptation, population differentiation and responses to environmental change.

This comprehensive five-day course combines theoretical instruction with hands-on analysis of genomic datasets, providing participants with practical skills for designing, conducting and interpreting GWAS analyses.

What you'll gain

  *   Understand the principles of Genome-Wide Association Studies (GWAS) and their applications in ecological and evolutionary research

  *   Explore genetic variation and the genomic architecture of complex traits

  *   Understand major genomic reference resources, including HapMap, the 1000 Genomes Project, HGDP and ERGA

  *   Understand genotype phasing and imputation

  *   Use PHASEIT2 and IMPUTE2 for phasing and genotype imputation

  *   Perform genotype quality control, including Hardy?Weinberg equilibrium testing, SNP call-rate filtering and minor allele frequency thresholds

  *   Conduct genome-wide association testing using PLINK and SNPTEST

  *   Apply linear and logistic association models

  *   Understand covariate adjustment and genome-wide significance thresholds

  *   Perform GWAS meta-analysis using GWAMA and METAL

  *   Process next-generation sequencing data using GATK

  *   Understand sequence alignment, duplicate handling, base quality score recalibration and variant calling

  *   Apply permutation testing and conditional association analyses

  *   Explore computational strategies for large genomic datasets

  *   Visualise association results using Manhattan plots and LocusZoom

  *   Use Principal Component Analysis (PCA) to investigate population structure

  *   Understand how population structure can confound genotype?phenotype associations

  *   Construct and validate polygenic risk scores

  *   Explore missing heritability, rare variants, gene?gene interactions and gene?environment interactions

  *   Develop reproducible workflows for analysing genomic variation and complex traits

Course format

  *   Dates: 9?13 November 2026

  *   Duration: 5 days, 5 hours per day ? 25 hours total

  *   Format: Live online

  *   Combination of theoretical lectures and hands-on computational exercises

  *   Practical analysis using R, Python and specialist genomic software

  *   Computational exercises using Google Colab and Posit Cloud

  *   Course code, datasets and presentation materials provided

  *   All sessions recorded

  *   30 days' access to recordings

  *   30 days of post-course email support

  *   Digital certificate of attendance

  *   Course fee: ?400

Who is this course for?

This course will be particularly relevant to researchers and postgraduate students working in:

  *   Molecular ecology

  *   Ecological genomics

  *   Evolutionary ecology

  *   Conservation genetics and genomics

  *   Population genetics

  *   Adaptation and environmental tolerance

  *   Quantitative genetics

  *   Ecological and evolutionary responses to environmental change

  *   Wildlife genetics

  *   Plant and animal genomics

  *   Genetic variation in natural populations

  *   Genotype?phenotype associations

  *   Complex trait genetics

  *   Bioinformatics and computational biology

It is particularly valuable for researchers interested in understanding how genetic variation contributes to ecologically important phenotypic traits, and how genome-wide association methods can be used to investigate variation within and among natural populations.

Participants should have a basic understanding of genetics, molecular biology and statistics, together with some familiarity with R and command-line computing. Previous experience with genomic datasets or population genetics is beneficial but not essential.

Why take this course?

Ecological research increasingly incorporates genomic data to investigate how organisms respond to their environments, adapt to changing conditions and maintain variation in important biological traits.

Many traits of ecological importance, including temperature tolerance, disease resistance, growth, reproductive characteristics and other life-history traits, are influenced by variation across multiple regions of the genome.

Genome-Wide Association Studies provide a powerful framework for identifying genetic variants associated with these complex traits, helping researchers investigate the genomic basis of phenotypic variation in natural populations.

For ecologists, these approaches can be particularly useful when studying adaptation to environmental gradients, differences among populations, genetic variation associated with ecological characteristics and the potential genetic basis of responses to environmental change.

However, analysing genome-wide associations requires careful consideration of genotype quality, population structure, relatedness, statistical significance and confounding factors. These issues are particularly important in ecological datasets, where individuals are often sampled from geographically or genetically structured populations.

GWAS01 provides practical training in the analytical workflow, from genomic data processing and quality control through association testing, visualisation and interpretation.

Participants will gain experience with established tools including PLINK, SNPTEST, GATK, GWAMA, METAL and LocusZoom, alongside approaches for genotype imputation, population structure correction and polygenic prediction.

The course also explores important challenges in complex trait genetics, including missing heritability, rare variants and gene?environment interactions, providing a broader understanding of the opportunities and limitations of GWAS in ecological and evolutionary research.

Although the course uses examples from evolutionary and broader genomic research rather than focusing exclusively on ecology, the analytical methods are directly applicable to ecological genomics, conservation genetics and studies of adaptive phenotypic variation.

By the end of the course, participants will have developed the skills needed to move from genomic datasets to robust association analysis and meaningful biological interpretation, providing a practical foundation for investigating the genetic basis of ecologically important traits.

Please feel free to share this course with colleagues, friends, research groups, labs, and others within your institute, organisation, or wider research network.

